{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## What was observed\n\nNothing. This ticket records an observation made by READING the code, not a failure seen in the wild. It is filed at P4 for that reason, and it should not be promoted without an actual observation.\n\n## The reading\n\nThe completion close path pins a ref without fetching, and the post-verify drift check then re-resolves the same pin:\n\n1. `src/rebar/_commands/transition_close.py:128-143` — `_pin_completion_ref(None)` resolves the default ref via `resolve_ref(\"HEAD\", fetch=False)`.\n2. `src/rebar/_commands/close_autoresume.py:259-279` — verification runs at that pin, `fetch=False`.\n3. `src/rebar/_commands/transition_close.py:310-323` — the signing drift check re-resolves that same pinned SHA, again with `fetch=False`.\n\n`src/rebar/opcert_service/jobs.py:176-187` pins the same way. `src/rebar/llm/gate_source.py:89-109` resolves `current_head_sha` with `fetch=False`, comparing against a local idea of `origin/main` rather than the remote tip.\n\nOn that reading, step 3 compares the tree against itself, so it would not report the local tree being behind the remote.\n\n## What is NOT established\n\n- **No false PASS has ever been observed.** The single incident in the wild (recorded on `8806-198c-e2d6-46ce`) was a false FAIL: the verifier could not see work that had merged. That is the safe direction.\n- **No attestation is known to have been minted over a stale tree.** Two signatures were produced after a workspace was manually fast-forwarded to the SHA they name — which is consistent with correct verification against a freshly advanced tree, and is not evidence of anything else.\n- Whether the drift check is *supposed* to catch this at all has not been checked. It may be scoped to catch the tree moving during verification, in which case comparing the pin against itself over that window is the intended behaviour and there is no defect here.\n\nThat last point is the one to settle first, and it costs a reading of the check's intent rather than any census.\n\n## Why this is not folded into 8806\n\n`8806-198c-e2d6-46ce` is a deployment defect with observed impact: the MCP code workspace is cloned once and never refreshed. Its fix advances that checkout and is independent of anything here.\n\n## If this is ever picked up\n\nStart by establishing intent: does the drift check exist to detect the tree moving during verification, or to detect verifying a stale tree? If the former, close this as not-a-bug. Only if the latter, and only after an actual stale-verification is observed, is there a defect to fix — and the fix would be for the check to compare against the authoritative remote tip, failing closed when that tip cannot be determined.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The intended scope of the post-verify drift check is established from the code and its history, and recorded on this ticket. Verified by reading that comment.\n- [ ] If the check is intended only to detect movement during verification, this ticket is closed as not-a-bug with that finding recorded. Verified by the close class.\n- [ ] If instead a defect is confirmed AND an actual stale verification has been observed, a test reproducing that observation fails against the current code. Verified by the recorded red output.\n\n## Out of scope\n\nA store-wide `verified-at-sha` census. An earlier draft of this ticket made that its first deliverable, which was inventing expensive work to substantiate a hypothesis that no observation supports. If a false PASS is ever observed, a census becomes justified then.","title":"Unverified reading: the completion close drift check may re-resolve its own pin, and its intended scope is unestablished"}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1789785219813846448,"uuid":"e415c169-0ce2-47a3-8d56-82c2c3ff8419"}